A||||7||Alex4mula||Help! Removing sail panel assy.||||5||
Z||000000||Alex4mula||02-25-2001||05:02 PM||||<BR>On 88 Formula; I'm removing the sail panel assembly to replace panels and painting the whole thing. I was able to take out the two plastic clips of the rear portion. What about the third atachment behind the Pontiac red logo? Is that another clip or a screw? My service manual shows a screw there but there is no access from behind. I'm scare of prying it off & break it if I'm not sure it is just a clip. Please help. Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||TheFiero64||02-25-2001||05:15 PM||||There is a nut. It is only accessible from the inside. you must remove the B pillar trim to reach it. You will need a socket and extension bar. I can't remember what size it was.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||gabriel||02-25-2001||05:55 PM||||If it's like mine, it's one of those crappy stamped steel nuts used to screw over a plastic stud.  Mine is 10mm & unless you use some way to stick the nut to the socket, it will drop into the quarter panel.  For D*** sure *do not* drop the socket, or you will be hearing it until you sell the car.||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||02-25-2001||06:19 PM||||See Paul McKibben's how-to instructions: <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/</A> <P>On my '85 SE, I was able to pry the forward clips off the sail panels without removing interior trim.  You need a long screwdriver, and it helps to have a good mental picture of what you're trying to do.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||02-25-2001||07:56 PM||||They are 10mm nuts but have an 11mm socket too. Some are flatten enough that a 10mm won't go over it. If the nut drops, you can usually fish it out at the bottom vent.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||danny boy||02-25-2001||10:46 PM||||Yeah, the guy who bought my '86 coupe got at least 4 10mm sockets with the car.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||02-26-2001||08:53 AM||||Thanks guys. I was able to take them out with Paul McKibben's method. Not hard at all.<BR>What was very painful was to take the adhesive off. Anyway, it's done. Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||TheFiero64||02-26-2001||12:04 PM||||I dropped a socket somewhere when working on my engine and I still have not found it. I hear it in the frame when I brake and accelerate.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||9
